questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where a modular Spring Boot platform dynamically loads and unloads client plugins via custom `ClassLoader` instances. During continuous delivery deployments, plugins are frequently upgraded, but after a few hours, the JVM crashes with `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`. A memory dump analysis reveals that thousands of instances of `PluginClassLoader` remain reachable despite having been discarded by the application framework.

You isolate the issue to the following shared framework utility invoked by long-running HTTP worker threads from the Tomcat thread pool:

java
@Component
public class PluginContextRegistry {
    private static final ThreadLocal<Map<Class<?>, Object>> contextCache =
            ThreadLocal.withInitial(HashMap::new);

    public void bindPluginService(Class<?> pluginContract, Object serviceInstance) {
        contextCache.get().put(pluginContract, serviceInstance);
    }

    public void clearThread() {
        // Intended cleanup before thread goes back to pool
        contextCache.get().clear();
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where reader threads in a high-throughput caching service periodically observe inconsistent state in domain objects. The cache is updated by background fetcher threads using double-checked locking without proper synchronization primitives:

java
public class ConfigurationRegistry {
    private ConfigHolder configHolder;

    public ConfigHolder getConfig() {
        if (configHolder == null) {
            synchronized (this) {
                if (configHolder == null) {
                    configHolder = new ConfigHolder("PROD", 5000);
                }
            }
        }
        return configHolder;
    }
}

public class ConfigHolder {
    public String env;
    public int timeoutMs;

    public ConfigHolder(String env, int timeoutMs) {
        this.env = env;
        this.timeoutMs = timeoutMs;
    }
}


Under high concurrency, reader threads occasionally observe `configHolder.timeoutMs` as `0` or `configHolder.env` as `null`.
